{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"stationary-absurd-gourami","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# The cutover withdrew a BLOCKING criterion's grounding tool, by prefix match, with no signal\n\n## Context / Problem\n\nPlan-review criterion **T1** (`facet: overlay-priorart`, `exec: AGENT`, `default_posture: **blocking**`)\ndeclares `\"web\": true` in the packaged routing registry\n(`src/rebar/llm/plan_review/criteria_routing.json`). Its reviewer prompt\n(`src/rebar/llm/reviewers/plan_review_T1.md`) instructs the model: *\"when the web-search tool is\noffered, USE it to ground prior-art claims in real, current sources.\"* T1 is the bright-line overlay —\nit fires on external integration, unfamiliar dependency, security/auth, a novel architectural pattern,\na perf/scalability target, or a migration.\n\nThe tool is attached only by this gate (`src/rebar/llm/anthropic_model.py`,\n`_anthropic_web_search_capabilities`):\n\n    if not web or not resolved.startswith(\"anthropic\"):\n        return None\n\n**Since the production review bot was cut over to Bedrock [rebar:eb6e-f43a-e86b-443a], `resolved` is\n`bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8`, which does not start with `anthropic`. So T1 now runs WITHOUT\nits grounding tool on every production plan review.** The live chain, each link verified:\n`criteria_routing.json` `\"web\": true` -> `criteria/model.py` `\"web\": bool(routing_entry.get(\"web\", False))`\n-> `plan_review/passes.py` `web=agentic and any(bool(c.get(\"web\")) for c in chunk)` -> `runner.py`\n`_anthropic_web_search_capabilities(resolved, web=req.web)` -> the prefix gate above.\n\n## Why this matters\n\n1. **It is a BLOCKING criterion.** T1 can block a plan. It now does so on weaker evidence — codebase\n   and model knowledge instead of current sources — for exactly the plans that cross a bright-line.\n2. **It is decided by PREFIX SHAPE, not capability.** The epic's standing rule is provider\n   qualification by REGISTRY MEMBERSHIP, never prefix matching, with zero exceptions — enforced for the\n   capabilities module by an attested guard. This site is the same anti-pattern in a different module,\n   and the cutover is what made it bite: the identical MODEL (Claude) reached through a different\n   PROVIDER loses a capability because of how its name is spelled.\n3. **It is SILENT.** No warning is logged and the signed verdict's `provider_provenance.capabilities`\n   records `native_structured_output`, `prompt_cache_style`, `supports_temperature` and\n   `supports_thinking` — but nothing about web search. So a reader of the verdict cannot tell that a\n   declared grounding tool was withheld. This is the failure shape the epic kept hitting: the guard\n   shares the bug's assumption.\n4. **It contradicts the epic's own premise.** Epic 061c asserts `first_class = full fidelity`, and\n   both Anthropic and Bedrock are first-class. This is a measurable fidelity difference between them\n   that no acceptance criterion covers.\n\n**Not a hallucination risk** — and this is the one piece of good news. T1's prompt handles absence\nexplicitly: *\"when it is not available, fall back to codebase and plan-text reasoning ... and do NOT\nfabricate web citations.\"* So the degradation is quiet quality loss, not invented sources.\n\n## The open question the fix depends on\n\n**Does Bedrock's Converse API support Anthropic's server-side `web_search` tool at all?** This is\nunresolved and MUST be answered by measurement before choosing a fix, not assumed either way:\n\n* **If Bedrock supports it** — the gate is simply wrong, and the fix is to attach the capability by\n  capability/profile lookup (the `ModelProfile`-derived path the seam already uses for caching and\n  structured output) rather than by `startswith(\"anthropic\")`.\n* **If Bedrock does NOT support it** — returning `None` is CORRECT behaviour, and the defect is\n  narrower but still real: the decision is reached by prefix shape rather than by a capability the\n  registry knows, and the withdrawal is invisible. The fix is then to derive it from the profile AND\n  to record it, so a verdict says the tool was unavailable.\n\nEither way the prefix match should go. Do NOT weaken\n`test_capabilities_module_still_has_no_provider_name_prefix_matching` to accommodate this.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/anthropic_model.py` — `_anthropic_web_search_capabilities`, the prefix gate.\n- `src/rebar/llm/capabilities.py` — most likely home for a profile-derived `supports_server_web_search`\n  capability, alongside the existing `prompt_cache_style` / `native_structured_output` decisions.\n- the `provider_provenance.capabilities` payload, so the outcome is observable in a signed verdict.\n\n## Testing\n\n- a test that with a `bedrock:`-qualified Claude model and `web=True`, the outcome matches the MEASURED\n  Bedrock capability — and that the decision does not consult the provider-name string. Verify RED by\n  reinstating a prefix match.\n- a test that the signed verdict's provenance records whether server-side web search was attached, so\n  the silent case cannot return. Verify RED by removing the field.\n- record the Bedrock measurement itself (a real call with the capability requested) as the evidence for\n  whichever branch the fix takes; \"not supported\" is an acceptable answer, an unmeasured assumption is\n  not.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] whether Bedrock supports Anthropic's server-side web_search is MEASURED and recorded, with the\n      call and its outcome, before the fix is chosen.\n- [ ] the web-search capability decision no longer consults a provider-name prefix; it derives from the\n      model profile / capability registry, consistent with the epic's zero-exceptions rule.\n- [ ] a signed verdict's `provider_provenance.capabilities` records whether server-side web search was\n      attached, so the withdrawal can never again be silent.\n- [ ] T1's behaviour on Bedrock is stated in the docs the reviewer prompt relies on, so a blocking\n      prior-art finding produced without web grounding is interpretable.\n- [ ] tests pin the above and are each verified RED by mutation.","id":"129e-2d88-cce2-492c","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Bedrock cutover silently removed server-side web search from blocking criterion T1, gated by provider-name prefix"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785808240534544001,"uuid":"c4bc6a9b-2964-4b02-94a1-814412e2971d"}